<div class="meta">LESSON 0031 · 2026-06-15</div>
<h1>Levels of Algorithmic Trading</h1>
<div class="why">
  <strong>Why it matters for this project:</strong>
  This repo spans <em>five</em> strategy levels — from daily-frequency Python ML all the way to nanosecond VHDL pipelines.
  Understanding where each file sits on the spectrum tells you what edge it's trying to capture,
  why it's designed the way it is, and what you'd need to move to the next level.
  Lesson 0011 explained the latency technology ladder; this lesson explains the <em>strategy</em> taxonomy on top of it.
</div>

<div class="prog-row">
  <div class="prog-bar"><div class="prog-fill" id="pbar"></div></div>
  <div class="prog-lbl" id="plbl">0 / 7 sections</div>
</div>

<!-- ── SECTION 1: The spectrum ──────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>1. The Spectrum at a Glance</h2>
<label class="sec-check" data-s="0"><input type="checkbox"/>Mark complete</label>

<p>Every algorithmic trading strategy can be placed on a single axis: <strong>decision frequency</strong>. How often does the strategy decide what to do?  That one number determines the edge it chases, the infrastructure it needs, and the competition it faces.</p>

<div class="spectrum">
  <div class="spec-bar">
    <div class="spec-seg" style="background:#94a3b8">L0<br>Daily</div>
    <div class="spec-seg" style="background:#60a5fa">L1<br>Hours</div>
    <div class="spec-seg" style="background:#4ade80">L2<br>Seconds</div>
    <div class="spec-seg" style="background:#facc15">L3<br>Milli&shy;seconds</div>
    <div class="spec-seg" style="background:#fb923c">L4<br>Micro&shy;seconds</div>
    <div class="spec-seg" style="background:#f87171">L5<br>Nano&shy;seconds</div>
  </div>
  <div class="spec-labels">
    <div class="spec-lbl">Quant/ML</div>
    <div class="spec-lbl">Stat Arb</div>
    <div class="spec-lbl">Market Making</div>
    <div class="spec-lbl">Low-Latency</div>
    <div class="spec-lbl">ULL CPU</div>
    <div class="spec-lbl">FPGA/ASIC</div>
  </div>
  <div class="spec-arrow">
    <span>← slower, cheaper, more capital needed</span>
    <span>faster, more expensive infrastructure →</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Three principles hold across the entire spectrum:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Faster signals decay faster.</strong> A daily-momentum signal might have a half-life of weeks. A nanosecond latency edge has a half-life measured in months before competitors match your speed.</li>
  <li><strong>Faster strategies need less capital, but more infrastructure.</strong> An L5 FPGA firm may run on $10M of capital but spend $5M on hardware, colocation, and connectivity per year.</li>
  <li><strong>Your competition shifts at every level.</strong> At L0 you compete with multi-billion-dollar quant funds. At L5 you compete with firms whose entire strategy is "be 1 nanosecond faster than you."</li>
</ol>

<!-- ── SECTION 2: Level-by-level ────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>2. Each Level in Detail</h2>
<label class="sec-check" data-s="1"><input type="checkbox"/>Mark complete</label>

<!-- L0 -->
<div class="level-card lc-0">
  <div class="level-header lh-0">
    <span class="level-badge lb-0">L0</span>
    <span class="level-name">Daily / Weekly Quant</span>
    <span class="level-tagline">This repo's Python core</span>
  </div>
  <div class="level-body">
    <dl class="level-grid">
      <dt>Decision cadence</dt><dd>Once per day (close of market)</dd>
      <dt>Signal type</dt><dd>Fundamental + statistical (returns, factors, ML features)</dd>
      <dt>Holding period</dt><dd>Days to months</dd>
      <dt>Edge source</dt><dd>Superior model (better features, better ML, less look-ahead bias)</dd>
      <dt>Infrastructure</dt><dd>Laptop / cloud VM; no special hardware needed</dd>
      <dt>Typical Sharpe</dt><dd>0.5–2.0 after costs (varies enormously by strategy)</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="level-desc">
      <p>This is where the vast majority of quant researchers start. You train a model on historical features (price momentum, fundamentals, sentiment), predict the next-day return, rank tickers, and hold a basket. The edge comes from <em>what you know</em> — a better model, a better dataset, better feature engineering.</p>
      <p>The main risks are look-ahead bias (you secretly used future data in training), overfitting (your model memorized noise), and alpha decay (other funds found the same signal). The infrastructure requirement is near zero: any Python environment works.</p>
      <p><strong>In this repo:</strong> <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code>, <code>walkforward_sim.py</code>, <code>predict_next.py</code>. The GBM model predicts <code>fwd_ret_1d</code>, selects the top-N tickers by predicted return, and holds for one day.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- L1 -->
<div class="level-card lc-1">
  <div class="level-header lh-1">
    <span class="level-badge lb-1">L1</span>
    <span class="level-name">Intraday Statistical Arbitrage</span>
    <span class="level-tagline">Exercise 03 (daily pairs) is the L0 version</span>
  </div>
  <div class="level-body">
    <dl class="level-grid">
      <dt>Decision cadence</dt><dd>Minutes to hours during market hours</dd>
      <dt>Signal type</dt><dd>Statistical (cointegration, mean-reversion, cross-sectional)</dd>
      <dt>Holding period</dt><dd>Minutes to end-of-day</dd>
      <dt>Edge source</dt><dd>Pair relationship more stable intraday; better execution precision</dd>
      <dt>Infrastructure</dt><dd>Low-latency co-located server; direct market access</dd>
      <dt>Typical Sharpe</dt><dd>1.5–4.0 (higher frequency = more diversification of bets)</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="level-desc">
      <p>Statistical arbitrage extends the pairs-trading idea to intraday: find cointegrated instruments, wait for the spread to widen, enter both legs simultaneously, exit when it reverts. Because you trade both sides (long + short), the strategy is market-neutral.</p>
      <p>Moving from daily to intraday changes what you need to worry about: transaction costs dominate (you might trade dozens of times per day), and you need execution fast enough to hit the spread at the entry price you modeled. The strategy in <code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/</code> runs at daily granularity — moving it intraday would require tick data and DMA connectivity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- L2 -->
<div class="level-card lc-2">
  <div class="level-header lh-2">
    <span class="level-badge lb-2">L2</span>
    <span class="level-name">Market Making</span>
    <span class="level-tagline"><code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code>, exercises 01–02</span>
  </div>
  <div class="level-body">
    <dl class="level-grid">
      <dt>Decision cadence</dt><dd>Per tick (microseconds to milliseconds, continuous)</dd>
      <dt>Signal type</dt><dd>Microstructure (bid-ask spread, order flow imbalance, inventory)</dd>
      <dt>Holding period</dt><dd>Milliseconds to seconds</dd>
      <dt>Edge source</dt><dd>Spread capture minus adverse selection; superior inventory risk management</dd>
      <dt>Infrastructure</dt><dd>Low-latency hardware, direct venue connectivity, co-location</dd>
      <dt>Typical Sharpe</dt><dd>3–10+ (many small, uncorrelated bets)</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="level-desc">
      <p>A market maker continuously posts both a bid and an ask, earning the spread when both sides fill. The enemy is <em>adverse selection</em>: a sophisticated counterparty knows the price is about to move against you and picks off your stale quote. The art is canceling quotes fast enough when the market moves, while capturing spread from uninformed order flow.</p>
      <p>The Avellaneda-Stoikov model in <code>solutions/02_market_maker/</code> is the textbook approach to optimal quote placement under inventory risk. The VHDL version in <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code> is an inventory-blind simplified version — the repo's L5 exercise.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- L3 -->
<div class="level-card lc-3">
  <div class="level-header lh-3">
    <span class="level-badge lb-3">L3</span>
    <span class="level-name">Low-Latency Arbitrage</span>
    <span class="level-tagline"><code>gradient_boosting/main.cpp</code>, exercise 04</span>
  </div>
  <div class="level-body">
    <dl class="level-grid">
      <dt>Decision cadence</dt><dd>Sub-millisecond (100 µs – 1 ms on a fast market data event)</dd>
      <dt>Signal type</dt><dd>Price discrepancy across venues or correlated instruments</dd>
      <dt>Holding period</dt><dd>Microseconds to milliseconds</dd>
      <dt>Edge source</dt><dd>Pure speed — arrive at the mispricing before it's corrected</dd>
      <dt>Infrastructure</dt><dd>C++ kernel bypass, co-location, microwave links between exchanges</dd>
      <dt>Typical Sharpe</dt><dd>5–50 (pure alpha, low variance, latency is the moat)</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="level-desc">
      <p>At this level the signal is often as simple as "the futures contract moved, the ETF hasn't yet — buy the ETF before it catches up." The edge is entirely in being <em>faster</em>. The ML model becomes less important; the kernel-bypass networking and co-location become everything.</p>
      <p>In this repo, <code>gradient_boosting/main.cpp</code> runs ONNX inference in C++ — the infrastructure for L3, but currently applied to a daily-frequency signal (L0). Exercises 04 (SPSC ring, O(1) MA), 06 (C++ order book), 07 (binary feed handler), and 08 (latency benchmark) are all the building blocks of L3.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- L4 -->
<div class="level-card lc-4">
  <div class="level-header lh-4">
    <span class="level-badge lb-4">L4</span>
    <span class="level-name">Ultra-Low-Latency (CPU)</span>
    <span class="level-tagline">Kernel bypass, DPDK, SmartNIC</span>
  </div>
  <div class="level-body">
    <dl class="level-grid">
      <dt>Decision cadence</dt><dd>1–100 µs tick-to-trade</dd>
      <dt>Signal type</dt><dd>Order book imbalance, spread dynamics, same-venue arbitrage</dd>
      <dt>Holding period</dt><dd>Microseconds</dd>
      <dt>Edge source</dt><dd>Kernel bypass + CPU isolation; faster than competitors at same venue</dd>
      <dt>Infrastructure</dt><dd>DPDK, CPU pinning, isolcpus, SCHED_FIFO, PGO/LTO compiled C++</dd>
      <dt>Typical Sharpe</dt><dd>10–100+ (very high, but competitive bar is extremely high)</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="level-desc">
      <p>L4 removes the operating system from the data path. Instead of kernel TCP/IP sockets (50–120 µs), the NIC DMA transfers market data directly into user-space ring buffers (DPDK / OpenOnload: 3–10 µs). The strategy code runs on a pinned, isolated CPU core that the scheduler never touches.</p>
      <p>The strategies at L4 often look simple — threshold rules, imbalance signals — because the complexity budget is consumed by the infrastructure. Most of the firm's engineering effort is in maintaining and tuning the transport layer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- L5 -->
<div class="level-card lc-5">
  <div class="level-header lh-5">
    <span class="level-badge lb-5">L5</span>
    <span class="level-name">FPGA / ASIC (Nanoseconds)</span>
    <span class="level-tagline"><code>fpga/*.vhd</code>, exercises 05 + 09</span>
  </div>
  <div class="level-body">
    <dl class="level-grid">
      <dt>Decision cadence</dt><dd>4–50 ns (deterministic, fixed pipeline cycles)</dd>
      <dt>Signal type</dt><dd>Same as L4 but pipeline-limited to simple arithmetic</dd>
      <dt>Holding period</dt><dd>Nanoseconds to microseconds</dd>
      <dt>Edge source</dt><dd>Wire-rate parsing; jitter-free latency; co-location close to the matching engine</dd>
      <dt>Infrastructure</dt><dd>Xilinx/Intel FPGA, custom NIC cards, 10/25/100 GbE direct fabric</dd>
      <dt>Typical Sharpe</dt><dd>Varies; the edge is reproducible until a competitor matches latency</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="level-desc">
      <p>An FPGA replaces the CPU entirely for the hot path. The market data parser, the order book update, and the order generation all happen in a deterministic sequence of pipeline stages clocked at 200–400 MHz. No OS, no scheduling jitter, no cache misses. The latency from last bit of inbound packet to first bit of outbound order is measured in tens of nanoseconds.</p>
      <p><strong>In this repo:</strong> <code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> (2-stage multiply-accumulate pipeline), <code>fpga/moving_average_trader.vhd</code> (shift-register MA with threshold strategy), <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code> (mid-price + fixed spread quoting). Exercise 05 writes testbenches; exercise 09 (proposed) adds inventory skew.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

