<p>Why slower signals live longer:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>L5 (nanoseconds):</strong> Your latency edge survives until a competitor co-locates closer, buys faster hardware, or rewrites a tighter FPGA pipeline. Often months to 1–2 years before the edge compresses.</li>
  <li><strong>L2 (market making):</strong> The MM business model has existed for decades but individual spread levels compress constantly. The edge is now in adverse selection prediction and inventory management, not just "post both sides."</li>
  <li><strong>L0 (daily ML):</strong> Momentum factors have existed since Jegadeesh-Titman 1993 and still work. Why? They're hard to scale (buying the same stocks pushes prices against you), and market structure keeps producing new price reactions to news. But alpha has declined from ~2% monthly to sub-0.5% as crowding increased.</li>
</ul>

<div class="box box-info">
  <h4>The crowding problem</h4>
  When many funds discover the same L0 signal and trade it at the same time (same ticker selection on the same day), they all buy together and push prices up before the signal can be realized. This is the primary reason daily quant strategies need to keep refreshing their signals and features — the current repo signal may already be partially crowded.
</div>

