<!-- ── SECTION 5: Often confused with ─────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>5. Often Confused With</h2>
<label class="sec-check" data-s="4"><input type="checkbox"/>Mark complete</label>

<h3>Frequency vs. holding period</h3>
<p>These are related but not the same. A strategy can <em>decide</em> frequently but hold for a long time, or decide rarely but hold very briefly. A high-frequency market maker makes thousands of decisions per second but average holding time is milliseconds. A monthly-rebalancing pension fund decides rarely and holds for months.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Property</th><th>Decision frequency</th><th>Holding period</th></tr>
  <tr><td>L0 quant (this repo)</td><td>1/day</td><td>1–30 days</td></tr>
  <tr><td>L2 market maker</td><td>Every tick (~1,000/s)</td><td>ms–seconds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>L3 latency arb</td><td>On correlated events (~100/s)</td><td>microseconds</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Latency vs. signal horizon</h3>
<p>From lesson 0011, latency is the time from tick-in to order-out. Signal horizon is how far into the future the strategy's prediction is valid. A daily quant has high tick-to-trade latency (orders aren't time-critical) but a long signal horizon (the prediction is about tomorrow). An L5 market maker has nanosecond latency but a signal horizon measured in microseconds. You need latency &lt; signal horizon to profit.</p>

<h3>"Algorithmic" vs "Quantitative" vs "High-Frequency"</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th><th>Where the repo fits</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Algorithmic trading</strong></td><td>Any trading where code executes the strategy — includes rule-based, execution algos, and everything below</td><td>Everything in this repo</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Quantitative trading</strong></td><td>Statistical / ML models generate signals; human researchers design the models</td><td>paper_trading_sim.py, walkforward_sim.py</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>High-frequency trading</strong></td><td>Strictly: holding periods &lt; 1 day, very high order rates, latency-sensitive. Some define it as sub-millisecond only</td><td>fpga/*.vhd, exercises 06–08</td></tr>
</table>

